# deploy.conf — values specific to THIS host (the only place one deployment differs from another).
#
# Copy to deploy/deploy.conf (gitignored, like the env file in /etc) and fill in for the machine you
# are deploying to, then run deploy/render-config.sh to generate its systemd units and Caddy site
# file from the committed templates. Nothing host-specific is baked into a tracked file, so moving
# to another machine is: edit this, re-render.
#
# The values below are illustrative defaults, not a live deployment. Change what you need to.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RUNTIME ACCOUNT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# What the API and loader processes run as. A system account: no password, no shell, no home, never
# logged into. It needs exactly four things — read the installed release, read the deployment env
# file, listen on APP_PORT, reach the database — and a flaw in the Node process yields those and
# nothing else.
#
# Keep it SEPARATE from whoever deploys. A deployer needs sudo (installing units, reloading Caddy is
# root's work); the service needs none of that. If one account did both, the service would run as a
# member of the sudo group, and anyone who compromised the process could wait for a password rather
# than needing one.
#
# One runtime account per service, rather than one shared by every service on the machine: a
# compromise should reach one service's credentials, not all of them.
#
# There is deliberately no setting here for the DEPLOY account. Deploying is done by a person with
# sudo — an administrator of this host, not an account created for this application. Per-application
# admin accounts are not a boundary: each has full sudo, so each can become root and reach every
# other one's files. Separate by person, for attribution; separate by service, for the runtime.
RUNTIME_USER=zfin-api

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where installed versions of the application live on the target.
#
# This folder is managed by deploy/install-release.sh, which keeps several versions side by side
# and a `current` shortcut pointing at the one that should be running:
#
#     /srv/zfin-data-api/
#       releases/
#         zfin-data-api-20260912T080000Z-a74c0ae/
#         zfin-data-api-20260913T193000Z-c852d8f/
#       current -> releases/zfin-data-api-20260913T193000Z-c852d8f
#
# The systemd units run from the shortcut, so rolling back is repointing it and restarting — the
# previous version is still on disk, complete.
#
# NOT a home directory. The runtime account has to read this, and a home directory is 0750 to its
# owner. The units also set ProtectHome=yes, which hides /home from them entirely.
#
# It holds no secrets — those are in /etc/zfin-data-api — so it does not need tight permissions.
# Let a deploy group write it and everyone read it:
#     sudo groupadd -f deploy && sudo usermod -aG deploy <you>
#     sudo install -d -o root -g deploy -m 2775 /srv/zfin-data-api
APP_DIR=/srv/zfin-data-api

# Absolute path to the Node binary (systemd has no nvm or PATH). Node 20+ is required by NestJS 11.
#
# A SYSTEM Node, not one under a user's ~/.nvm. Same reason as APP_DIR: the runtime account cannot
# read another user's home, and ProtectHome=yes hides /home from the unit besides. Installing it
# system-wide also means the version that builds the code is the version that runs it.
NODE_BIN=/usr/bin/node

# The public hostname Caddy serves and obtains a Let's Encrypt certificate for. DNS must already
# resolve this name to this machine before the Caddy step, or the certificate cannot be issued.
#
# REPLACING AN EXISTING DEPLOYMENT? Put a STAGING name here first — the live name still resolves to
# the old machine, and pointing this at it before cutover only has Caddy chase a certificate for a
# host that answers somewhere else. Verify the new deployment against the old one, then re-render
# with the real name once DNS moves.
SITE_ADDRESS=zfin-api.example.org

# The localhost port the API listens on. Must match PORT in the deployment env file;
# render-config.sh refuses to render if the two disagree. Check what is free:  ss -ltn
APP_PORT=3480
